---
/*
 * The community's fix for CSA-2026-01: a script that checks a device for the
 * known traces of the malware. The script lives in
 * https://github.com/admonstrator/glinet-csa-2026-01; this page is its guide.
 */
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import Footer from '../../../components/Footer.astro';
import Terminal from '../../../components/Terminal.astro';
import { loadContent } from '../../../lib/content';

const { site, generatedAt, security } = loadContent();
const advisory = security.advisories.find((a) => a.id === 'CSA-2026-01')!;
const url = new URL(`${advisory.url}fix/`, site.url).href;
const repo = 'https://github.com/admonstrator/glinet-csa-2026-01';
const rth = 'https://github.com/RemoteToHome-io/gl-security-incident-2026';
const steps = [
  {
    title: 'Check',
    text: 'Only reads and prints a report. It changes nothing.',
    command: 'wget -q https://app.gl-i.net/csa-2026-01-fix -O gl-helper-triage.sh ; sh gl-helper-triage.sh',
  },
  {
    title: 'Save the evidence',
    text: 'Writes an archive to /tmp, which is in RAM, so copy it to your computer before you reboot. It contains your configuration, possibly with Wi-Fi and VPN keys, so don’t post it anywhere.',
    command: 'sh gl-helper-triage.sh --archive',
  },
  {
    title: 'Clean up',
    text: 'Only if the check found something. Saves the evidence again, stops the malware, removes its files and autostart entries, then checks again.',
    command: 'sh gl-helper-triage.sh --fix',
  },
];
const labels = [
  { label: 'IOC', meaning: 'A known trace of the malware', fix: 'Removes it' },
  { label: 'PERSIST', meaning: 'A changed rc.local or root crontab without a reference to the malware', fix: 'Resets it only when an IOC was found' },
  { label: 'REVIEW', meaning: 'An unusual change, not proof of an infection. Check whether it is yours.', fix: 'Never touches it' },
  { label: 'INFO', meaning: 'Context, for example files installed by a package', fix: 'Never touches it' },
];
const markers = ['/etc/glinet/.cache/gl-helper', '/etc/glinet/.cache/gb', '/etc/config/.gl/gl-helper', '/etc/config/.gl/gb'];
---

<Layout
  title={`Community fix for ${advisory.id}: check a device for the gl-helper malware · gl-i.net (unofficial)`}
  description="A community script that checks a GL.iNet router or KVM device for the malware from GL.iNet's security notice of 20 November 2026, saves the evidence and cleans up. Not a GL.iNet tool."
  url={url}
>
  <SiteHeader site={site} security={security} page="advisory" />

  <main id="top">
    <article class="container sa-article fix">
      <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/">Community Apps</a><span class="sep">/</span><a href="/security/">Security advisories</a><span class="sep">/</span><a class="mono" href={advisory.url}>{advisory.id}</a><span class="sep">/</span><span aria-current="page">Community fix</span>
      </nav>

      <header class="sa-header fix-head">
        <div>
          <p class="adv-eyebrow"><span>Community fix</span>{' '}<span>Not a GL.iNet tool</span></p>
          <h1>Check your GL.iNet device for the gl-helper malware</h1>
          <p class="sub">
            On 20 November 2026 GL.iNet said that its cloud remote management had been abused to install malware on
            some routers and KVM devices. Its own quick check looks at running processes. The community built a script
            that runs the same check and also looks at the files on a device. It saves the evidence and removes the
            known traces on request.
          </p>
        </div>
        <img src="/img/csa-2026-01-fix.svg" width="240" height="200" alt="A seal resting on a patched-up router" />
      </header>

      <section class="fix-warn" aria-labelledby="h-warn">
        <h2 id="h-warn">Read this first</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><b>This is community work, not a GL.iNet tool.</b> GL.iNet has not reviewed or endorsed it. Use it at your own risk.</li>
          <li><b>Run it before you restart, reset or update the device.</b> Some traces exist only until the next restart, and the KVM firmware 1.10.2 for the RM1PE deletes the malware’s files at boot without keeping a copy.</li>
          <li><b>It does not replace a factory reset or GL.iNet’s security firmware.</b> The script finds and removes only the traces we know of.</li>
          <li><b>In a business network, wait.</b> Talk to your cyber insurer or incident response provider before you change or reset the device. <a href={`${advisory.url}#community`}>Why</a></li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section id="run" class="do-box" aria-labelledby="h-run">
        <h2 id="h-run">Run it</h2>
        <p>Log in to the device over SSH as root. You can <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/gl-helper-triage.sh`}>read the script&nbsp;↗</a> first.</p>
        <ol class="fix-steps">
          {steps.map((s) => (
            <li>
              <b>{s.title}</b>
              <p>{s.text}</p>
              <Terminal command={s.command} label="root@device" />
            </li>
          ))}
        </ol>
        <p class="adv-fine">On an infected device <code>netstat</code> can be replaced, and GL.iNet staff says that running the replacement starts the malware. The script never calls it and reads the connections from the system directly.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="share" aria-labelledby="h-share">
        <h2 id="h-share">Help us see who was hit</h2>
        <p>
          At the end the script offers to send a short anonymous report to the community, even when it found
          nothing: reports from clean devices show which models and firmware versions were not hit. It says what the
          report contains and sends it only after you answer <code>y</code>; <code>v</code> shows the complete report
          first. With <code>--no-report</code> it never asks.
        </p>
        <p>
          The report says which model and firmware version the device has, when it was checked, which known traces
          were found and, for the reported remote shell, when it started and which port it used. For the malware’s own
          files and for replaced system tools it adds a fingerprint (SHA-256), the size and the time of the file, so
          that we can tell variants apart.
        </p>
        <p>
          It also contains what the attacker left, as text: the lines of changed autostart files (rc.local, the root
          crontab) that are not in the firmware image, the rules of the firewall chain the attack used, and the command
          lines of the malware’s processes. <b>Lines you added to those files yourself are part of this.</b> The script
          shows these lines word for word before it asks. Read them, and answer <code>n</code> if you see a password or
          token of yours.
        </p>
        <p>
          The report contains no host name and no other file. Other changes on the device are only counted. The
          sender’s address is not stored.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="report" aria-labelledby="h-report">
        <h2 id="h-report">Reading the report</h2>
        <div class="sa-table-wrap fix-table">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Label</th><th>Meaning</th><th>What <span class="mono">--fix</span> does</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {labels.map((l) => <tr><td><code>{l.label}</code></td><td>{l.meaning}</td><td>{l.fix}</td></tr>)}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>Exit codes: <code>0</code> clean, <code>1</code> traces found, <code>2</code> fixed and clean, <code>3</code> fix incomplete, <code>4</code> error.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="traces" aria-labelledby="h-traces">
        <h2 id="h-traces">What it looks for</h2>
        <p>The traces come from the cleanup GL.iNet shipped in the KVM firmware RM1PE 1.10.2. That cleanup runs when one of these files exists:</p>
        <ul class="unknowns">{markers.map((m) => <li><code>{m}</code></li>)}</ul>
        <p>
          It also runs <a href={`${advisory.url}#check`}>GL.iNet’s quick check</a> for a running process
          <code>crond -i /tmp/.log</code>, and looks for the remote shell that forum users and RemoteToHome Consulting
          found on GL-AXT1800 routers: a process that uses <code>/tmp/f</code> and connects to
          <code>170.39.226.219</code>. GL.iNet staff says to
          treat a router with such a shell as compromised. The pipe <code>/tmp/f</code> counts too: it stays after
          the shell was stopped, until the next restart. The script also runs the further checks staff recommends:
          processes such as <code>nc</code>, <code>socat</code> or <code>/bin/sh -i</code>, and connections held by a
          shell.
        </p>
        <p>
          Every running program is compared with the read-only firmware image. A program that was started from
          <code>/tmp</code>, is not part of the firmware or differs from it is listed as <code>REVIEW</code>, or as
          <code>INFO</code> when a package installed it. All of this only shows what is running right now.
        </p>
        <p>
          The report lists the open network connections with their program, and every line of the firewall
          configuration that is not in the firmware image. Your own port forwards appear there too. It also shows the
          running firewall rules that redirect traffic.
        </p>
        <p>
          The script also finds system tools that were replaced (for example <code>netstat</code>), processes started
          from these paths, and autostart entries that point to them: crontabs, <code>rc.local</code>, init scripts and
          the lists of files a firmware update keeps. <a href={`${repo}#-indicators`}>All details are in the README&nbsp;↗</a>
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="after" aria-labelledby="h-after">
        <h2 id="h-after">Afterwards</h2>
        <ul class="unknowns">
          <li>Keep the evidence archive.</li>
          <li>KVM: install GL.iNet’s security firmware. Only the 1.10.2 build for the RM1PE removes the malware’s files by itself; the builds for the RM1 and RM1V2 do not.</li>
          <li>Router: update <b>without keeping settings</b>. The malware hides in <code>/etc/config</code>, which an update with kept settings preserves.</li>
          <li>A factory reset wipes everything the malware could have written and is the safe option on both.</li>
          <li>Check the computer attached to a KVM, and the devices behind a router.</li>
          <li>Change the device password, your GL.iNet cloud account password and your SSH keys.</li>
          <li>
            If the device was hit, everything it held a key for is exposed too. Remove the router’s VPN profile on the
            server or with the VPN provider and create a new one, and remove the router’s SSH keys from every machine
            that trusts them. <a href={`${rth}#if-you-find-something`}>RemoteToHome’s recovery steps&nbsp;↗</a>
          </li>
          <li>KVM: after an update that keeps settings, the password, the SSH keys and everything under <code>/etc/kvmd/user</code> are still the old ones. Change them and look for keys and scripts you did not add.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section id="more" aria-labelledby="h-more">
        <h2 id="h-more">More</h2>
        <ul class="sources">
          <li><a href={advisory.url}>Advisory {advisory.id}</a><p>What happened and what GL.iNet recommends</p></li>
          <li><a href={rth}>RemoteToHome’s findings on the incident&nbsp;↗</a><p>How the remote shell got in, what the KVM firmware does, and how to recover a device</p></li>
          <li><a href={repo}>Source code and README on GitHub&nbsp;↗</a><p>All options, the script for running it from your computer, tests and limits</p></li>
          <li><a href={`${repo}/issues`}>Report a problem&nbsp;↗</a><p>Bugs and false alarms. Never attach an evidence archive.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <p class="adv-fine sa-fine">
        By <a href="https://github.com/Admonstrator">Admon</a> and the community:
        <a href="https://remotetohome.io">RemoteToHome Consulting</a> and users of GL.iNet’s forum contributed findings
        and checks. An independent community project. Provided as-is
        under the MIT licence, without any warranty. Get firmware only from GL.iNet.
      </p>
      <p class="adv-fine">
        Thanks to <a href="https://remotetohome.io">RemoteToHome Consulting</a> for the analysis of the remote shell
        and of the KVM firmware, for the recovery steps and for how to read the firmware build, to the forum users
        kosrkosr and TakashiSasaki for the first reports of the shell, to AdamK for the connection checks, and to
        everyone who sent a report or posted their output.
      </p>

      <nav class="sa-pager" aria-label="Back">
        <a href={advisory.url}>← {advisory.id} · {advisory.title}</a>
      </nav>
    </article>
  </main>

  <Footer site={site} generatedAt={generatedAt} />

  <script>
    import '../../../scripts/alerts.ts';
    import '../../../scripts/security.ts';
  </script>
</Layout>
